00:00:25.802 --> 00:00:28.010
PROFESSOR: So today we're
going to talk about cooking

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.710
and stoves and
charcoal and then there

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.630
will be plenty of
time afterwards

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:36.020
to work on your project stuff.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.130
Ken is here already.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:39.260
[INAUDIBLE] is around here.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.490
And Amelia will
be coming at 2:00,

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.255
so everyone has a
mentor to work with.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:48.350
Yeah.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.110
So we did the labs in reverse.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.840
So charcoal, we did
the lab on Wednesday.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.370
And even though
it's warmer today,

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.930
I think the rain would have
made it even less pleasant.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.990
So I think that
was the right call.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.550
So we'll go through
that a little bit more.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.160
Yeah, and I just
explained everything else.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.670
So let's start just by
talking about cooking

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:08.790
in developing countries.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:13.895
So what do people cook and what
are their needs for cooking?

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.178
Soups.

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:18.720
Sweet potato.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.690
And how do they
typically prepare that?

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.070
There's a wide variety.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.750
But what are some of
the cooking things

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:42.627
that you need to be able to
do from an energy perspective?

00:01:42.627 --> 00:01:43.481
STUDENT: Boil water.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.200
PROFESSOR: Anything else?

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.543
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:51.543 --> 00:01:52.210
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.756
So that would be oven.

00:01:55.756 --> 00:01:57.083
STUDENT: Grinding.

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:57.750
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:03.700
OK.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.520
Cool, what else?

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.889
Meat.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.740
And what do you do
with meet energy wise?

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.000
I'm sorry?

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.710
Open fire.

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.820
Anything else?

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.300
So stew.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.020
Anything else?

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.270
What else?

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:27.770
Rice.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:32.500
And that would be--

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.840
is that captured here?

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:35.340
Yeah.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.805
Anything else?

00:02:36.805 --> 00:02:37.305
Beans.

00:02:41.893 --> 00:02:43.310
STUDENT: Do you
need high pressure

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.890
for anything [INAUDIBLE]?

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.050
PROFESSOR: Pressure
cookers exist.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.070
You don't see them a lot.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.152
STUDENT: You don't need
pressure for anything, do you?

00:02:55.152 --> 00:02:56.130
PROFESSOR: No.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:02:59.710
OK.

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:03.760
So this is a modest list.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.920
But some things
that are missing are

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.690
bread is a staple
in a lot of places,

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:17.953
whether it's a tortilla or an
Indian flatbread or-- yeah?

00:03:17.953 --> 00:03:19.620
STUDENT: No, I mean,
I'm just wondering.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.030
[INAUDIBLE] mentioned
the pressure cooker.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.300
How do you cook meat
without a pressure cooker?

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.050
Like how would you
reach that temperature?

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.830
Like if you wanted
to cook it in water,

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:31.510
wouldn't the water just boil
if you don't pressurize it?

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.360
Won't it just evaporate
if you don't--

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.768
PROFESSOR: So most
people don't boil meat.

00:03:37.768 --> 00:03:39.310
STUDENT: Oh, so they
don't need water

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.393
when they're preparing it.

00:03:40.393 --> 00:03:42.370
[INAUDIBLE]

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:46.033
PROFESSOR: So yeah, so
I'm not saying that--

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:47.950
this might be a confusing
way to present this.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:49.660
I'm not saying that
every one of these

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.600
requires every one of these.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.150
Just that these are some of
the foods that you're cooking

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.920
and these are some of the ways
that you're preparing them.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.730
So I'm just trying to make sure
that all of these are captured

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.970
or that all the methods are
captured but not linking--

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:07.570
yeah, because I would
be reluctant to eat

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:10.750
boiled meat straight
without any other preparing.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.320
Though I think cabbage and
corned beef and cabbage

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.040
is boiled.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.876
So I think you can do it.

00:04:16.876 --> 00:04:18.383
STUDENT: Vegetables.

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:19.300
PROFESSOR: Vegetables?

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:19.800
Yeah.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:28.150
Yep.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:33.040
So meat is kind of uncommon
on most people's staples.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.800
So you get it infrequently.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.920
Rice and beans or other combos
that make whole proteins

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.500
are pretty common.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.250
And a lot of people
live on rice and beans

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:45.190
pretty much every
day of their lives

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.300
with some tortillas sometimes,
in Nicaragua certainly.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.500
Vegetables.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.340
Oftentimes you grow some
vegetables and they supplement,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:58.390
but you'll live on a
staple of rice and beans

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:00.100
a lot of where we're working.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:04.000
And in terms of the cooking,
so for all of these,

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.870
there's the range
of you need low heat

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.460
or you could need high heat.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.350
You can need an oven.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:20.320
So talking about direct
fire hitting the pan,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:27.910
hitting the food versus an oven
is going to be more convective.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:37.130
And then-- yeah, and people are
pretty picky about the control

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:39.950
of their fire for most of
the-- or the heating source

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:41.160
for most of these options.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.710
And so you'll see--
the people who

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.470
go to [? Grupo Fenix ?]
will definitely see this.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.450
I don't know about
also [INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.900
But in [? Grupo Fenix, ?] a
lot of women or a few women

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:55.850
have gas stoves, but they still
maintain their old style adobe

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.900
wood because they will only
cook their tortillas on wood.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:05.870
They just can't
cook on gas in a way

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:07.940
that they believe makes
an acceptable tortilla.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.910
So that is one of the things
that I want to emphasize today,

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.410
I'm going to keep
coming back to it,

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:15.170
is that people
understandably are

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:18.440
very sensitive to how they cook
and have very long standing

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:19.062
traditions.

00:06:19.062 --> 00:06:21.020
They are cooking the same
way their moms cooked

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:22.610
and their moms and their moms.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:26.810
And so proposing to someone to
cook in a radically different

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:29.720
way is challenging and
is something people

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.480
are going to resist for
really understandable reasons.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:37.310
So we've got the low heat,
the high heat, and the oven.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:39.035
That sort of captures it all.

00:06:39.035 --> 00:06:40.910
With boiling water, you
need high enough heat

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:44.360
that you can
actually boil water.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.060
And each of those is
critical to most people.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:50.960
You can't cook with 0,
with only one, ideally.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.140
And so people who even just cook
on three stone fires with wood

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.180
just get really
good at regulating.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:59.240
The oven is the optional
one that's the bonus.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.958
But people who
cook with wood get

00:07:02.958 --> 00:07:05.000
really good at regulating
their fire so that they

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.540
can get their low heat
or their high heat,

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:08.450
depending on what they
want, because most people

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:09.575
need to be able to do both.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.800
So what fuels are
typically used?

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:26.290
Wood, yep.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.570
[LAUGHS]

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.820
I'm not asking radically
crazy questions.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:33.970
I think there's another one that
might come out in your mind.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:34.570
Yes.

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:37.425
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:37.425 --> 00:07:38.050
PROFESSOR: Yep.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:43.655
STUDENT: Propane.

00:07:43.655 --> 00:07:44.280
PROFESSOR: Yep.

00:07:47.753 --> 00:07:48.753
STUDENT: Kerosene maybe.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:59.590
PROFESSOR: Some people also
cook with just general biomass.

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:07.780
So that might be corn
stalks or hay or similar.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.270
It's not a great fire, but
if it's what you've got,

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:11.410
it's better than nothing.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:13.900
Because one thing to
note on most of these is

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.960
can get away with not cooking
some of your vegetables

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.930
depending on how you
fertilize them if you did.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:25.570
But you can't cook bread or you
can't skip cooking all of these

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.210
and expect to be
able to digest them.

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:30.760
So cooking is a non-negotiable.

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.898
It's our basic
human need, which is

00:08:33.898 --> 00:08:36.190
part of why there's such a
high level of risk aversion,

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:38.770
because you're asking people
to change what they know

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:43.120
is the thing that's keeping them
alive and their family alive.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:49.090
So some things to just keep in
mind generally about cooking

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:54.850
is that all modern fuel increase
with income except kerosene.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:09:01.570
So people tend to
go from wood, well,

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:07.450
from dung to wood to charcoal
to propane and kerosene.

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:09.520
But then, well, to kerosene
and then to propane.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.740
But people tend to stick
to propane for a while

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:15.340
because it's readily available.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:20.080
And people get used to it.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.810
In urban areas,
unsurprisingly, biomass

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:27.070
is less common than
in rural areas.

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.800
And in those rural areas, people
often just stick with biomass

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.050
forever because--

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:34.810
so even if you can afford
it, it's still easier

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:37.930
to get wood that you can chop
down basically in your backyard

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:39.850
than to go get propane.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:41.890
So you'll stick with it.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:44.050
And then another
thing to think about

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:46.210
is that even if people
have enough income,

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:49.678
if you can't get your
LPG or electric stoves

00:09:49.678 --> 00:09:51.470
or whatever, if you
don't have electricity,

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:52.887
you don't have the
infrastructure,

00:09:52.887 --> 00:09:54.910
you're not going to switch.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:57.160
And then we often
think about what I just

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:59.980
said about going into your
quote "backyard" to gather wood.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.980
But a lot of people in
relatively rural areas

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.350
purchase their wood
rather than collecting it,

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.830
either for time reasons
or because it's actually

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:11.770
there's so much deforestation
that it's really hard to get

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:14.290
the wood both in
terms of distance

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:17.440
and in terms of legality,
because often it's cutting down

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.750
wood on other people's property.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:24.790
And so you are avoiding doing
the directly illegal thing

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.620
and just purchasing it.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:30.020
It's less common in Central
America where we'll be going.

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:33.160
But in a lot of regions,
cutting down wood for fuel

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:35.320
is now a crime.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:37.510
But there is no
alternative cooking method,

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:41.030
and so it still happens,
but now it's illegal.

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:45.730
And so it's become more
challenging in that respect

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:47.650
and more expensive.

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:51.820
In terms of how people
cook, it really ranges.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:55.210
In places that have
similar levels of wealth,

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:56.920
you can see huge variations.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:11:02.350
So there's LPG, electricity,
kerosene, and wood.

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:06.160
In Guatemala, whereas
here in Ghana,

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.900
wood is really used very
little and charcoal really

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:12.980
is used quite a bit more.

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:16.660
So it varies place to
place what people use.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:21.580
And a lot of that has to do
with how far away the wood is

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:27.320
and therefore how expensive it
is and a lot of other factors.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:31.540
But you should not assume,
this goes back to week one,

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:33.970
everyone cooks with wood
in a developing country.

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:37.870
It's very location
specific and even location

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.920
within the country specific
what people are doing.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:45.010
The challenges with
fuel are that it

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:49.420
can be up to a third of people's
income just to pay for fuel.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:51.880
And a third of your
income is kind of insane

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:56.320
if you imagine spending a
third of your income on fuel.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:58.360
The poorest people in
the US are the ones

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.340
who are paying the most
are paying less than 1%

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:01.900
of their income on cooking fuel.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:04.330
So if you imagine
going from something

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.090
that cost you 1% of
your income to 30%,

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:09.450
that's getting
really substantial.

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.950
And people who are collecting
it are sometimes in some areas

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:14.380
spending up to seven hours
a day collecting firewood,

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:18.160
which is basically the
same impact on your family

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:20.110
that if you're spending
seven hours a day,

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:23.320
you're not having any
time to do anything else

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:28.900
but get enough fuel
for cooking your food.

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:31.300
And then just
looking at the energy

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.280
densities of all of these.

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:37.600
So wood charcoal
and the charcoal

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:41.740
that we made on Wednesday is
very similar to wood charcoal.

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.300
You can get really
good wood charcoal

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:46.068
that's better than ours
and you can get really

00:12:46.068 --> 00:12:47.860
junky wood charcoal
that's worse than ours.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:52.150
So we're about in
the middle there.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:55.110
Everything else
that's a biomass,

00:12:55.110 --> 00:13:01.380
because it's not
compressed and/or processed

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:02.640
is going to be worse.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:06.420
Dung is quite poor, both on
energy density as well as

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.970
health because you can
imagine burning dung is worse

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:11.740
than burning anything else.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:15.540
And then heating oil,
all the things that we're

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.850
used to generally,
gas or electric, well,

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.350
electric doesn't
really fit in here,

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:22.030
but gas is certainly
a whole lot better.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:23.490
And so of course,
people are going

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.190
to switch to here if
they can, but it's

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.510
expensive and challenging.

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:32.210
And just from a
visual standpoint,

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:34.680
these are all the cooking
options to think about.

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:41.010
So there's electricity,
propane, wood.

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.650
These are typically wood ovens.

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:44.510
Dung.

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:48.650
This is the Jiko stove that I'll
show a little bit later, which

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:51.050
can be used with charcoal.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:53.570
There's cooking,
and then there's

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:56.722
a variety of different cooking
stoves that you can cook with.

00:13:56.722 --> 00:13:58.430
And then this is just
a three stone fire.

00:13:58.430 --> 00:14:02.030
So you can see there's three
stones here to support your pot

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:07.700
and then you feed your logs
in this way to cook your food.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:10.727
So that's just a quick
overview on cooking.

00:14:10.727 --> 00:14:12.060
We're going to get into it more.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:15.860
But I wanted to switch gears
and do a quick estimation.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:17.060
And it says in teams of two.

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:19.880
If you want to do it in three
or four, that's fine too.

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:23.790
But what's the energy required
to boil a liter of water

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:25.445
and what's the
efficiency to do so?

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:31.020
Go.

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:49.280
Yeah.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:53.030
So I want to talk a little bit
about design considerations

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:54.720
for stoves.

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:57.750
Some of you may be
working on those projects.

00:14:57.750 --> 00:14:59.450
So what do you think
you have to worry

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:01.595
about when you design a stove?

00:15:05.336 --> 00:15:06.340
STUDENT: Air flow.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.040
PROFESSOR: Air flow, yes.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:10.660
Definitely.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:13.810
What else?

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:15.270
STUDENT: Insulation.

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:16.540
PROFESSOR: Yep.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:18.890
STUDENT: The environment.

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:20.630
PROFESSOR: The
environment you're in.

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:22.672
STUDENT: As in close
proximity, your environment,

00:15:22.672 --> 00:15:25.420
making sure there is
nothing flammable nearby.

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:27.300
[INAUDIBLE]

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.800
PROFESSOR: So safety.

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:34.660
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:35.924
PROFESSOR: Yep.

00:15:35.924 --> 00:15:36.890
STUDENT: Size?

00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:37.910
PROFESSOR: Size?

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.164
Yeah.

00:15:40.164 --> 00:15:41.232
STUDENT: Heat source.

00:15:41.232 --> 00:15:42.190
PROFESSOR: Heat source.

00:15:46.905 --> 00:15:47.530
STUDENT: Price.

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:04.150
PROFESSOR: Anything else?

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:05.620
STUDENT: Usability.

00:16:17.213 --> 00:16:17.880
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:20.675
So if you're cooking
outside, certainly.

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:26.140
I think I'm below the
magic line, aren't I?

00:16:30.530 --> 00:16:31.830
Anything else?

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:32.750
STUDENT: Aesthetics.

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:33.375
PROFESSOR: Yep.

00:16:41.042 --> 00:16:44.978
STUDENT: Convenience as in how
long it takes, time to use.

00:16:48.914 --> 00:16:51.400
[INAUDIBLE] falls
under usability but

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:52.952
whether or not it's intuitive.

00:16:52.952 --> 00:16:56.167
Usability is already
written [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:56.167 --> 00:16:57.000
PROFESSOR: So it is.

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:00.270
All right.

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.550
Well, this is a good start.

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:05.579
To just give you a sense
of the list that I came up

00:17:05.579 --> 00:17:08.880
with with another
person, there's

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:11.490
the insulation and
the thermal mass.

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:13.890
And this is actually
a complicated issue,

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:18.180
because in some cases,
like with an oven,

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:20.369
you want really, really
good thermal mass.

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:25.530
So you heat up that thermal
mass and then that thermal mass

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:29.160
allows you to cook longer,
whereas for a charcoal stove,

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:30.690
you don't want a
big thermal mass.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.120
You don't want necessarily
0 thermal mass.

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:35.220
But if you have too
much thermal mass,

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:38.220
the charcoal will
use all its energy

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:41.310
in transferring it to the
stove and you won't be

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.590
able to cook very much with it.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.510
Air flow is another
important consideration.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:48.760
The reason the Jiko worked
so poorly on Wednesday

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:51.210
was actually because the
door never got opened.

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:55.170
That was my fault. I
realized that after when

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:56.760
I was cleaning up
then we never opened

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.280
the door, which meant
there was no airflow, which

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:03.600
pretty much limits the ability
of the fuel to get hot.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:04.380
The volume.

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:07.020
So yeah, which we mentioned,
how much you're going to cook

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:07.870
is huge.

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:10.260
People have really
varying needs there.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:11.770
Robustness to water.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:14.670
So the Jiko, one of the
challenges with it is it's

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:17.250
made of ceramic, which makes
a really nice insulator.

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:20.130
But as we talked about,
if you pour water

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:23.400
onto it to prevent the--

00:18:26.841 --> 00:18:31.158
to put out the charcoal, the
ceramic is going to crack.

00:18:31.158 --> 00:18:33.450
And also if you drop it, the
ceramic is going to crack.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.640
But boiling water it's
pretty common for some water

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:37.270
to run over the
edge of the stove.

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:39.990
And so that alone
can cause cracking.

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:43.530
The cost of the device
is really critical,

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:45.720
as this is such a
fundamental need.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:46.810
Everyone needs one.

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:50.250
And so if it's unaffordable,
that's pretty problematic.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:51.828
Longevity is another concern.

00:18:51.828 --> 00:18:53.370
A lot of these metal
stoves that I'll

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:57.120
be showing in a second don't
have a lot of longevity,

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:59.250
but they are affordable.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:01.020
And so Paul [INAUDIBLE]
would be really

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:03.300
happy with that balancing act.

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:07.830
Other people would be less
happy, but it is an issue.

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:08.910
The heating options.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:12.180
So ideally you could
have some magic stove

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:14.130
where you could use any fuel.

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:21.240
You could use charcoal or wood
or regular biomass or propane

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:22.983
or electricity or
whatever you could get.

00:19:22.983 --> 00:19:24.150
You'd have this magic stove.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:27.690
But that's not very realistic
from the price standpoint.

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:31.110
but people do like to be able
to cook with a range of fuels.

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:33.480
Ease of use is
definitely important.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:35.550
If it's hard to light
or hard to keep the fire

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.630
lit or hard to get your pot on.

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:40.410
It's pretty unfortunate stove.

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:42.990
Emissions is another thing
to think about in terms

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:46.320
of where does the smoke go.

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:49.350
A lot of times it goes
straight into the kitchen.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:51.780
You'll see in the
homes in Nicaragua,

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:54.280
the stoves look a lot like this.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:56.220
And even when they
do have a chimney,

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:59.410
it's rare that all three holes
for the pots are covered.

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:02.612
And so the chimney
in that situation

00:20:02.612 --> 00:20:03.570
doesn't work very well.

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:05.910
A lot of the smoke escapes.

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:06.900
Efficiency.

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:11.460
So how much of the heat
actually gets to cook as opposed

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:13.410
to doing something else.

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:16.230
Style, aesthetics are
all pretty important.

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:19.020
People do want their
Kitchens to be attractive.

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:24.090
And so while we're not
talking about a criteria

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:26.220
similar to does it look
like the stainless steel

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:29.250
kitchen in a high end kitchen
or a professional stove

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:30.870
in a high end kitchen
in the US, it's

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.970
still a really critical
thing to think about.

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:36.610
And a really junky looking
stove will not do very well.

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:40.080
And then flexibility for
cooking with different fuels

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.730
or different types of
foods, et cetera, et cetera.

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:43.860
So I'm just going to--

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:47.820
if we can turn it around
to go to the back corner

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:51.990
and show you a wide
variety of stoves.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:54.180
So this is one of the
stoves we used yesterday.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:57.060
And it's a pretty
simple wood stove that's

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:58.830
a relatively common design.

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.910
So you can see that it has the
upper surface for cooking with.

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:04.840
I'm not really sure
what these decorative--

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:06.590
if these are just
decorative or if there's

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:08.430
some purpose to them.

00:21:08.430 --> 00:21:11.460
But it did prevent us from
using a certain pot yesterday,

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:13.685
so it was a frustrating element.

00:21:13.685 --> 00:21:16.163
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:16.163 --> 00:21:16.830
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:20.670
So it might be to hold a
special type of pot, absolutely.

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:22.890
And then a place
to catch the ash

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:28.350
is pretty handy as well so
that the ash doesn't just

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:30.720
fall on the ground.

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:35.160
And it has plenty of holes
to allow for airflow.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:41.430
The Jiko, These two
are called Jikos,

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:50.800
which is a design of a stove
that was developed in Africa,

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:52.290
in Kenya I believe.

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:57.420
And the Jiko has a ceramic
part that holds your fuel

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:01.350
and also acts as an insulator
and then metal everywhere else.

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:04.020
This one was not
made as carefully

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:09.690
and may be a knockoff
of this one, which

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:12.900
you can see the ceramic liner
is actually thicker on this one.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:16.290
So it works a little better.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.530
It has the door that allows
you to control airflow.

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:22.380
So that's a big improvement over
this one and the insulation.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:26.220
It has these three
pieces, which allow

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:29.370
you to hold a smaller pot.

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:32.487
It has little handles that
may or may not work too well.

00:22:32.487 --> 00:22:34.320
This is pretty heavy
for such small handles,

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:36.278
so I don't like actually
carrying it like that.

00:22:36.278 --> 00:22:37.980
But it's a quite improved stove.

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:41.340
And in terms of efficiency, I
believe it cuts fuel use down

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:42.810
by like 30%.

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:46.080
So it's significant.

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:49.690
This is an interesting
stove that I don't know,

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:53.290
can you see this at all Eric?

00:22:53.290 --> 00:22:55.290
OK, great.

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:58.770
So this is a knockoff Jiko.

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:02.400
And so you can see that it
kind of looks like the Jiko

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.590
but it's got a bigger door.

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:06.840
It has some really monstrous
handles that are actually

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:08.550
easier to use than those.

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:09.870
So that's a big improvement.

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:12.930
But it actually has so
much thermal mass in it

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:15.360
that it gets to that issue of
there's so much thermal mass

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:18.450
that you use all your
fuel heating the stove

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:19.320
rather than cooking.

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:23.880
And so it takes forever
to cook with this.

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:25.780
Like a ridiculous
amount of time.

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:29.190
And so it's one of those,
well, if this much insulation

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:31.200
is good, this must
be better assumption

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:32.290
that is really common.

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:34.200
We actually with our
charcoal we found

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:37.260
that if we make a charcoal--

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:39.690
that people like-- or
I'll talk about it more.

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:42.990
But a bigger piece of charcoal
that they can cut in half

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:45.540
and make into the same
pieces of charcoal that

00:23:45.540 --> 00:23:48.330
are identical in size, but
they prefer the bigger size.

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:49.320
And same with stoves.

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:51.900
People tend to bigger is
better is the assumption.

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:58.500
These next four I'm going
to show our all stoves I

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:00.630
bought in Nicaragua.

00:24:00.630 --> 00:24:06.630
So this one is cool
because it was a tire hub.

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:09.600
So a nice use of recycling.

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:11.580
These legs allowed
it to actually

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:15.200
be at this height,
which is pretty great.

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:17.670
However, for bringing
it back on the airplane,

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:20.700
I cut off the legs.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:25.230
Mostly charcoal in
Nicaragua is used just

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:30.090
for frying up food at
places called fritangas.

00:24:30.090 --> 00:24:33.100
And so they're just done
on the street corners

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:33.990
and as a sales thing.

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:36.010
It's rare to see these
in people's homes.

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:38.250
This is one, can even tell
what this is made out of?

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:46.780
So it's actually a gas cylinder
that got cut up probably

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:47.350
when it died.

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:49.090
And then rebar is the grating.

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:54.940
This one is really,
really simple

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:56.380
and just used out
of sheet metal.

00:24:56.380 --> 00:24:57.280
It's galvanized.

00:24:57.280 --> 00:25:00.670
So if you use it inside, it's
going to make people sick,

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:02.710
because the galvanized
metal gives off

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:04.340
fumes that are not so great.

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:06.130
And then this one is
an interesting design

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:09.280
that's pretty common,
usually made out of a--

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:10.463
it's usually bigger.

00:25:10.463 --> 00:25:12.880
It's like a 55 gallon drum,
but I couldn't bring that home

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:14.230
on the airplane.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:16.180
And this one is
interesting because it

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:18.340
allows you to use charcoal
or wood because you

00:25:18.340 --> 00:25:20.260
can feed the wood in
this way like would

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:22.010
in a traditional adobe stove.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:26.215
And so it's nice that it does
have that flexibility compared

00:25:26.215 --> 00:25:26.965
to all the others.

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:36.470
And all of them
generally come painted

00:25:36.470 --> 00:25:39.500
and then the paint all chips
away as you cook with it.

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:41.990
But that does speak to
the aesthetics interest

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:44.270
that people want to buy a
nice looking painted stove

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:46.380
even if the paint
doesn't last very long.

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:48.080
It helps them sell.

00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:48.800
Yeah?

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:50.930
STUDENT: One of the
ones that we [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:54.560
last year was really easy
to make with sheet metal.

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:58.490
Is that really important
in a device or [INAUDIBLE]??

00:25:58.490 --> 00:26:01.670
PROFESSOR: So what
that team identified

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:03.590
was that it was
important that this

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:06.540
be made by the women locally.

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:09.350
And so therefore, they
decided that a single piece

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.500
of sheet metal was going
to be easier for the women

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:16.370
to make locally than making
it out of many pieces.

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:18.812
You as a team, if you end
up working on a project,

00:26:18.812 --> 00:26:20.270
may come to a
different conclusion.

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:21.937
That's the conclusion
they came up with.

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:26.516
Yeah?

00:26:26.516 --> 00:26:29.650
STUDENT: Do they have
access to welding?

00:26:29.650 --> 00:26:31.330
PROFESSOR: So in
Nicaragua there's

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:34.630
plenty of people who well,
mostly with oxyacetylene

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:36.280
or stick welding.

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:39.420
But in the rural
villages, there aren't.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:43.330
And so for example, in
where Grupo Fenix is,

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:46.060
there isn't a welder in the
village, in the community.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:48.760
But if you went up to Ocotal,
you can find people who weld.

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:51.938
So there's definitely
welding capability.

00:26:51.938 --> 00:26:53.230
You should consider that local.

00:26:53.230 --> 00:26:56.945
But it's not something that the
women in the village could do.

00:26:56.945 --> 00:26:59.320
And so that's why they didn't
come up with a welded stove

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:01.150
was because they wanted to be
something that the women could

00:27:01.150 --> 00:27:02.230
do themselves.

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:12.130
So two really good resources are
cookstove.net, which goes more

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:14.950
into the theory of
cookstoves, and Aprovecho,

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:18.760
which is a huge organization
that does a lot of stove design

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:19.600
and testing.

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:21.340
And they have a lot
of great resources

00:27:21.340 --> 00:27:25.683
about how to design stoves,
how to test stoves, et cetera.

00:27:25.683 --> 00:27:27.475
Are there any other
questions about stoves?

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:32.630
OK.

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:36.530
So I'm going to talk
some more about charcoal.

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:40.640
So 2 and 1/2 billion
people cook with biomass.

00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:43.530
So that's the market
we're talking about,

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:46.190
as opposed to people cooking
with gas or electricity.

00:27:46.190 --> 00:27:51.020
And it's generally people living
on less than $2 or $3 a day,

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:54.260
though there is some
wide range on that.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:58.850
And there's really no
viable alternative.

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:01.550
I'll talk about solar
cookers a little later.

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:04.100
Propane fuel is just
unavailable and same

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:08.130
with the grid in these regions.

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:11.960
And so cooking with biomass,
which I got into a little,

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:13.620
has some real problems.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:15.800
So there's 1 million
and 1/2 people

00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:18.540
who die every year from
indoor air pollution,

00:28:18.540 --> 00:28:21.560
and that's generally
associated with cooking

00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:24.410
inside the home,
smoking inside the home.

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:26.300
There's other things
that also contribute,

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:29.220
but indoor air pollution
from cooking is a huge one.

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:30.703
And it's mostly
women and children

00:28:30.703 --> 00:28:32.120
who are the ones
tending the fires

00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:33.950
and taking the biggest hit here.

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:37.220
So there was a study
done that estimated

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:40.970
that if everyone who currently
cooks with wood or dung

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:44.687
and straw and biomass that's
raw switched to charcoal,

00:28:44.687 --> 00:28:46.520
half of those lives
would be saved right off

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:49.760
the bat with no added
ventilation, no other changes,

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:52.730
just switching from wood to
charcoal because it's that much

00:28:52.730 --> 00:28:53.330
cleaner.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.730
But then you get into
the environmental issue

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:57.680
of we're talking
about regions that

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:01.670
can be facing 6% to 10%
deforestation rates in areas

00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:03.920
that are up to 98% deforested.

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:07.310
And so if you encourage
everyone to switch to charcoal,

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:09.500
you're going to be
using up more wood

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:13.063
and you're going to decimate
the environment even further.

00:29:13.063 --> 00:29:14.480
And as I mentioned
before, there's

00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:16.400
also a poverty aspect
to cooking fuel

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:18.320
in terms of how much
money and/or time it

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:20.690
takes to be able to cook.

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:24.920
So the charcoal that we
talked about on Wednesday

00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:27.440
has some benefits in
that it burns as cleanly

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:28.790
as wood charcoal.

00:29:28.790 --> 00:29:31.070
It's using a true waste.

00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:33.320
The complicated part
about using a true waste

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:36.860
is that in every region,
some of the possible wastes

00:29:36.860 --> 00:29:37.940
are not true wastes.

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:43.520
And so corncobs and stalks
can be used in farms

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:45.890
or any of these materials
can be used in farms

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:48.260
to replenish the soil basically
except sugarcane, which

00:29:48.260 --> 00:29:50.270
really has no nutritive
value once you

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:52.230
press the sugar out of it.

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:53.930
And so in places
where people are

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:55.592
using that to
replenish the soil,

00:29:55.592 --> 00:29:57.050
it's a really bad
idea to encourage

00:29:57.050 --> 00:29:59.390
them to switch to
making charcoal from it

00:29:59.390 --> 00:30:01.280
because they're going
to decimate their land

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:02.720
and the land is so valuable.

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:07.670
However, in most farms that
are at the subsistence level,

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:09.890
you can find some
biomass that's rotting.

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:11.840
And so that's the
biomass we target.

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:17.690
So that's why we try
to have a process that

00:30:17.690 --> 00:30:19.860
works with a huge
variety of waste

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:21.890
so that you can use a
wide variety of waste

00:30:21.890 --> 00:30:23.392
and only use the
stuff that's waste.

00:30:23.392 --> 00:30:25.100
The nice thing about
using the true waste

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:26.933
is that it tends to be
rotting in such a way

00:30:26.933 --> 00:30:29.210
that it's producing a
lot of methane gas, which

00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:32.600
is one of the worst
greenhouse gases

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:36.513
and worse for the environment
than burning the material.

00:30:36.513 --> 00:30:38.930
And so they're actually areas
where you get carbon credits

00:30:38.930 --> 00:30:42.740
just for burning sugarcane waste
rather than letting it rot.

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:45.950
And so here rather than
just helping the environment

00:30:45.950 --> 00:30:49.700
by burning it, we're using
that fuel to cook with

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:54.180
as well as not getting the
methane in the environment.

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:57.740
So just to give you a sense
of where this project is,

00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:00.890
so a few years ago, we
got a $200,000 grant

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:03.140
from the World Bank
to make charcoal.

00:31:03.140 --> 00:31:05.660
And so we trained more
than 1,000 people in Haiti

00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:10.310
in the method and we have some
workshops or ateliers in Haiti

00:31:10.310 --> 00:31:12.650
that are making
the charcoal now.

00:31:12.650 --> 00:31:18.110
Typically the costs associated
with the charcoal or just

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:20.240
the drum and the press.

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:24.530
And so the drum is anywhere
from $10 to $30 in a country

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:28.580
and the press generally
is about $2 to make.

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:31.250
If someone's telling you
they're going to make it for 10,

00:31:31.250 --> 00:31:33.830
they're ripping you
off, so push back.

00:31:33.830 --> 00:31:37.490
Because most people do
when you talk to welders,

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:41.300
most people talk about
how expensive it is.

00:31:41.300 --> 00:31:44.120
But if you push, $2 is
a pretty standard price

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:47.490
we found almost everywhere.

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:50.780
So the process that we
went through yesterday

00:31:50.780 --> 00:31:55.250
was we took the raw waste, wrote
it into a drum, lit it on fire,

00:31:55.250 --> 00:32:00.650
covered it, then you're in an
anaerobic environment for about

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:03.980
two hours, and then you have
your charcoal finds, which you

00:32:03.980 --> 00:32:08.210
crush in the bag in a bag
method and the rice husks mixed

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:11.480
with the binder made
from yucca, briquette it,

00:32:11.480 --> 00:32:14.240
and then you can put
the briquettes back

00:32:14.240 --> 00:32:16.730
in the drum to harden
them after they've dried,

00:32:16.730 --> 00:32:19.080
and then you can cook with them.

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:24.290
So I just want to make
sure that you guys are all

00:32:24.290 --> 00:32:28.430
comfortable with this method,
because you'll be doing it.

00:32:28.430 --> 00:32:31.925
Most of you will be doing
it having only done it once.

00:32:31.925 --> 00:32:37.782
And so I just want to make
sure you are feeling confident.

00:32:37.782 --> 00:32:39.990
And so that's why I'm
reviewing it in so much detail.

00:32:39.990 --> 00:32:41.480
So when you load
the drum, you need

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:43.272
to remember to have
the stick in the middle

00:32:43.272 --> 00:32:44.360
to make your chimney.

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:47.037
You can use the fuses at the
bottom to help light the drum.

00:32:47.037 --> 00:32:49.370
And it works really well to
light from the bottom rather

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:50.667
than the top.

00:32:50.667 --> 00:32:52.250
If you just light
from the top, you'll

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:56.270
get much less effective burning.

00:32:56.270 --> 00:32:58.310
When you're covering
up the drum,

00:32:58.310 --> 00:33:00.290
again, first you see this smoke.

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:03.890
And actually, we saw very
little of the just white smoke.

00:33:03.890 --> 00:33:05.210
We saw mostly yellow smoke.

00:33:05.210 --> 00:33:07.020
The just white smoke is water.

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:09.380
And so if you're getting
a ton of just white smoke,

00:33:09.380 --> 00:33:12.120
that means your
waste is too dry.

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:15.410
You're almost never going
to see it as dry as we had

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:18.020
on Wednesday, because that--

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:19.950
too wet, excuse me.

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:20.450
Too dry.

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:23.150
So you almost never see it as
dry as we had on Wednesday,

00:33:23.150 --> 00:33:26.060
because that has been sitting
in a heated, incredibly

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:28.620
dry environment at
MIT for about a year.

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:30.770
And so that helps it
dry out at a level

00:33:30.770 --> 00:33:32.848
that you're never going
to see in the field.

00:33:32.848 --> 00:33:34.640
That's why it was
infected with cockroaches

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:37.370
because of all those--

00:33:37.370 --> 00:33:41.940
because it was spending so
long in the bowels of MIT.

00:33:41.940 --> 00:33:43.345
So you will see
some white smoke.

00:33:43.345 --> 00:33:44.720
But if that's all
you're getting,

00:33:44.720 --> 00:33:46.678
that's an indicator that
your stuff is too wet.

00:33:46.678 --> 00:33:49.730
Or if the drum just won't
catch on fire at all.

00:33:49.730 --> 00:33:51.680
And then you get
the yellow smoke

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:54.290
and then you get the
flame and then you

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:57.320
wait some period of time.

00:33:57.320 --> 00:34:00.530
And I'm sorry I can't give you
a seven minutes answer for how

00:34:00.530 --> 00:34:03.320
long you wait until you switch.

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:06.290
But things to look
for are remember

00:34:06.290 --> 00:34:08.420
how it looked like this,
but then you'd get puffs.

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:10.076
Of the yellow smoke
and so you want

00:34:10.076 --> 00:34:11.659
to wait until you're
no longer getting

00:34:11.659 --> 00:34:14.719
those puffs of yellow smoke,
because those are areas that

00:34:14.719 --> 00:34:16.699
haven't gotten any
heat yet that are now

00:34:16.699 --> 00:34:18.770
starting to catch on fire.

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:22.052
And then later on what
cued me to cover it up

00:34:22.052 --> 00:34:23.719
was we were starting
to get black smoke.

00:34:23.719 --> 00:34:25.880
And the black smoke
tends to mean that we're

00:34:25.880 --> 00:34:27.898
burning everything really fast.

00:34:27.898 --> 00:34:29.690
And so that's why we
needed to cover it up.

00:34:29.690 --> 00:34:32.150
But it is a judgment
call and it is tricky.

00:34:32.150 --> 00:34:34.820
And so there's no easy way.

00:34:34.820 --> 00:34:38.630
Everything from the humidity
to the temperature to the type

00:34:38.630 --> 00:34:39.800
of the way--

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:41.280
what was in the drum before.

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:42.863
That's another thing
to think about is

00:34:42.863 --> 00:34:44.719
if when you get an oil
drum, you don't know

00:34:44.719 --> 00:34:46.000
where you're getting it from.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.600
And so it may have oil in it.

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:50.205
And so you may need
to do like a pre

00:34:50.205 --> 00:34:51.830
burn with just a
little bit of material

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:53.719
to burn out whatever
junk is in the drum

00:34:53.719 --> 00:34:58.160
so that you have a clean-ish
drum to use for carbonization,

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:00.830
because you wouldn't want
oil impregnated briquettes,

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:04.550
because that would
burn pretty nastily.

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:09.410
And you can do side
by side carbonization

00:35:09.410 --> 00:35:11.870
if you have a few
drums, which is nice.

00:35:11.870 --> 00:35:14.762
And then covering it up, just
doing it relatively quickly

00:35:14.762 --> 00:35:16.220
but also carefully
so you don't get

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:19.295
burned, because the drum is
really hot at that point.

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:26.160
For making the binder,
just remembering

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:30.168
that you're going for this
really snot-like consistency.

00:35:30.168 --> 00:35:32.460
So checking and make sure
you get it before you use it.

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:34.050
Because if you
don't, the briquettes

00:35:34.050 --> 00:35:37.590
will completely fall apart,
and that's problematic.

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:40.470
And then using the right
amount so that you actually get

00:35:40.470 --> 00:35:48.400
a good briquette is important.

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:51.570
And then briquetting--
so I just wanted

00:35:51.570 --> 00:35:54.790
to show you a couple of other
briquette press examples.

00:35:54.790 --> 00:35:58.308
So the presses we used
yesterday are really simple.

00:35:58.308 --> 00:35:59.850
And I'll talk about
how we got there.

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:03.480
But there's a cup, an
ejector pin, and a plunger.

00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:05.948
And the block is
just because we don't

00:36:05.948 --> 00:36:07.740
have tables with holes
in them around here,

00:36:07.740 --> 00:36:09.970
but it's pretty easy to find
some of hole you can use.

00:36:09.970 --> 00:36:11.428
So you don't actually
need a block.

00:36:11.428 --> 00:36:15.290
But every team should plan
to bring a press with you.

00:36:15.290 --> 00:36:17.040
And then ideally, you
can make them there.

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:20.010
But it's nice to have
one to show as an example

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:21.480
to your welder.

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:23.430
You can leave it
there if you need to.

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:26.610
In Haiti, our colleagues
got tired of making

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:29.250
one briquette at a
time, so they came up

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:31.050
with this press that
makes four at a time

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:34.050
and then has a plate that slides
out sort of like our old style

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:36.690
press that I'll show
you in a minute to make

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:37.830
four briquettes at a time.

00:36:37.830 --> 00:36:40.660
The problem is you're
increasing the area,

00:36:40.660 --> 00:36:42.160
so you're distributing
the pressure.

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:44.430
And so you really have
to wail on this press

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:46.590
to make briquettes well.

00:36:46.590 --> 00:36:48.840
And 2009, they made
an extruding device

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:51.480
to make briquettes, which is
nice but a lot of material.

00:36:54.125 --> 00:36:55.500
And just when you
briquette them,

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:58.770
make sure you're hammering with
enough force that you actually

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:01.470
get well compacted briquettes
or else they won't-- when they

00:37:01.470 --> 00:37:03.990
dry, they'll fall apart.

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:07.050
So I want to talk a little bit
about how the briquette press

00:37:07.050 --> 00:37:10.560
came to be, because I
think a lot of D-Lab,

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:12.630
you work on a project
and then you're done.

00:37:12.630 --> 00:37:14.130
You hand off to the
community maybe.

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:17.520
And it seems like that's
all that it takes.

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:21.450
And at least in this case,
and I think in a lot of cases,

00:37:21.450 --> 00:37:26.080
there's more required to
get to a good product.

00:37:26.080 --> 00:37:29.338
So initially we made
briquettes just by hand,

00:37:29.338 --> 00:37:30.630
as you can see in this picture.

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:33.960
And you can imagine compressing
that material hard enough

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:36.300
to get a really solid
briquette was pretty painful

00:37:36.300 --> 00:37:37.800
on the hands, and
it really didn't

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:41.550
make very good briquettes
even if you were super strong

00:37:41.550 --> 00:37:42.750
compressing the briquettes.

00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:45.870
So then we looked at
buying a briquette maker.

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:49.440
And the mechanized ones that we
could find were about $8,000.

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:52.050
And it's entirely inappropriate.

00:37:52.050 --> 00:37:55.410
And so there was a bunch of
work done by Jessica [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:55.410 --> 00:37:58.020
to figure out the best method
for actually making briquettes.

00:37:58.020 --> 00:38:01.570
And she honed in on
impact farming them.

00:38:01.570 --> 00:38:06.100
And so she designed a
briquette maker here.

00:38:06.100 --> 00:38:08.280
And so this is one of
the earliest prototypes

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:11.190
that had a handle.

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:12.857
Actually that's the
handle for the door.

00:38:12.857 --> 00:38:14.232
Let me show you
the next picture.

00:38:14.232 --> 00:38:15.400
I think it's easier to see.

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:20.010
So you load your
material in the top

00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:21.510
and then it falls to the bottom.

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:22.440
This door is closed.

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:26.040
Then you put the plunger
in, close it with a pin,

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:27.900
hit it a few times,
and then when you open,

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:31.600
this plate at the bottom has
a pin sticking out the back.

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:34.740
And so it hits the back pin and
that pushes the briquette out

00:38:34.740 --> 00:38:35.830
so you can grab it.

00:38:35.830 --> 00:38:39.280
So it's a really nice design and
it made four to five briquettes

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:39.780
a minute.

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:41.113
So we were pretty happy with it.

00:38:41.113 --> 00:38:43.450
But it was of slow
and frustrating.

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:46.200
And so the first improvement
was to do a time study

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:49.590
to figure out what was taking
so long and how to improve it.

00:38:49.590 --> 00:38:52.140
And everything was
taking a long time,

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:54.570
but ejecting was
taking the most time.

00:38:54.570 --> 00:38:56.850
Loading was taking a while.

00:38:56.850 --> 00:39:01.620
And so someone that
Amy Smith worked with

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:05.430
came up with an improvement,
which got us to six

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:06.820
to eight briquettes per minute.

00:39:06.820 --> 00:39:09.240
And so you can see that
some of the improvements

00:39:09.240 --> 00:39:11.610
were instead of loading
from the very top, which

00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:15.960
required a lot more precision,
you loaded from The side.

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:18.807
And for ejecting, instead
of having a special ejection

00:39:18.807 --> 00:39:20.640
mechanism, which required
a lot of cleaning,

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:23.190
you just pulled this played out
and then dropped the briquette

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:23.700
down.

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:25.530
And then we actually--
well, actually I

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:26.760
can show you the presses.

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:27.600
They're over here.

00:39:33.320 --> 00:39:38.410
So this one that you can see
down here, it has this plate.

00:39:38.410 --> 00:39:41.500
And so the briquette
drops down there

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:43.420
like that plunger is trying to.

00:39:43.420 --> 00:39:44.920
And then when you
close this drawer,

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:46.920
it actually pushes the
briquette out of the way.

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:48.550
So it's a little bit mechanized.

00:39:48.550 --> 00:39:55.070
And so it helps speed
things up considerably.

00:39:55.070 --> 00:39:56.680
And so we were
really happy with it.

00:39:56.680 --> 00:40:00.400
But it was also a
relatively expensive design

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:03.580
because this plate
had to be quite thick.

00:40:03.580 --> 00:40:07.340
And this is a really expensive
piece of metal just right here.

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:10.600
And so we were pretty
concerned about the prices

00:40:10.600 --> 00:40:12.140
of some of these elements.

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:16.330
And so we had another person who
does a lot of work in Guatemala

00:40:16.330 --> 00:40:18.190
take a look at it
and redesign it.

00:40:18.190 --> 00:40:19.690
Unfortunately, I
wasn't able to find

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:22.840
one of these presses
in D-Lab today.

00:40:22.840 --> 00:40:25.660
But he just made some
subtle improvements

00:40:25.660 --> 00:40:28.600
so that that big
plate was cut down

00:40:28.600 --> 00:40:33.640
until it was just as wide as
the diameter of this shaft.

00:40:33.640 --> 00:40:36.220
And then everything else
was made with angle iron.

00:40:36.220 --> 00:40:39.830
An angle iron is relatively
cheap and available everywhere.

00:40:39.830 --> 00:40:42.970
And so that made it a
whole lot more affordable

00:40:42.970 --> 00:40:46.090
for our partners.

00:40:46.090 --> 00:40:47.690
Yeah, there are a
variety of changes.

00:40:47.690 --> 00:40:51.930
And so you can see
just looking at it,

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:55.510
there's a bit less metal
here is the biggest change,

00:40:55.510 --> 00:40:57.784
and that improved things a lot.

00:40:57.784 --> 00:41:07.665
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
fully solidified.

00:41:07.665 --> 00:41:09.165
There's going to
be some loss there.

00:41:09.165 --> 00:41:10.050
PROFESSOR: There is.

00:41:10.050 --> 00:41:14.790
That was not, I would say,
very significant compared to--

00:41:14.790 --> 00:41:17.850
actually this method, I think,
the method we have now is

00:41:17.850 --> 00:41:19.920
there's more waste that
we just do it on the tarp

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:22.800
so that we collect that waste.

00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:25.480
So we still weren't
happy with the price.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:28.290
And so we ended up
radically redesigning it

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:30.900
by removing all of
the material basically

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:34.830
to just the most basic elements.

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:37.800
And we started with
a circular design.

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:41.550
And that still stands, but
we've also added a square design

00:41:41.550 --> 00:41:45.960
so that if you have cylindrical
stock, you can use it.

00:41:45.960 --> 00:41:50.040
And if you don't, if you have
square stock, you can use it.

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:51.870
One of the nice things
about square stock

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:54.510
is that it's a little bit
more flexible in terms

00:41:54.510 --> 00:41:58.290
of these parts, the
two parts that you

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:02.860
need to fit into the press.

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:04.590
You don't need to
cut a careful circle.

00:42:04.590 --> 00:42:06.850
You can just cut a square
and that's a lot easier.

00:42:06.850 --> 00:42:08.340
And then same on the bottom.

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:10.780
You can just weld
these pieces on.

00:42:10.780 --> 00:42:15.570
So we took that to the field and
we got a few different designs

00:42:15.570 --> 00:42:17.410
back as improvements.

00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:22.590
So this one is just used
sheet metal that's bent around

00:42:22.590 --> 00:42:23.910
and then welded on the edges.

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:26.880
And so that further reduced
costs, because it's thinner.

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:29.130
It's a thick sheet
but not as thick.

00:42:29.130 --> 00:42:33.810
And then this group used
angle iron you can see and put

00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:36.940
four pieces of angle iron
together and welded the edges,

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:43.200
which requires a talented
welder, but if you have one

00:42:43.200 --> 00:42:45.610
is a really nice
way to do things.

00:42:45.610 --> 00:42:48.690
And so there's a whole
variety of little improvements

00:42:48.690 --> 00:42:50.490
that are still being made.

00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:53.970
But generally, we're very
happy with this press.

00:42:53.970 --> 00:42:56.820
Now, it's interesting to note
that some of our partners

00:42:56.820 --> 00:43:00.720
still prefer this press, as I
mentioned, because of the whole

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:02.280
bigger is better issue.

00:43:02.280 --> 00:43:03.990
They just really like
this because they

00:43:03.990 --> 00:43:07.860
feel like it's sturdier, even
though it's 10 times the cost

00:43:07.860 --> 00:43:12.600
and has no additional value in
terms of speed or labor comfort

00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:13.650
or anything like that.

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:15.150
So that's been one
of our challenges

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:17.460
is the people who
saw this press first,

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:20.100
they still want to use
it because they prefer it

00:43:20.100 --> 00:43:21.400
despite the cost difference.

00:43:21.400 --> 00:43:23.400
And so that's one thing
we're trying to work out

00:43:23.400 --> 00:43:27.570
is how do we get that
cachet into this press that

00:43:27.570 --> 00:43:30.640
is so much more affordable.

00:43:30.640 --> 00:43:32.640
So all that said,
and this is a lot

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:34.650
of years of effort of
improving and improving

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:36.070
in small and big ways.

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:41.430
And so it's just worth thinking
about all the different ways

00:43:41.430 --> 00:43:44.460
that a product
needs to be improved

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:47.010
past the initial prototype,
which is the stage that you'll

00:43:47.010 --> 00:43:47.820
be getting to.

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:52.560
So I wanted to make a
couple of other comments

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:54.720
about running a training,
because you guys

00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:56.220
will be doing--

00:43:56.220 --> 00:43:58.770
at least two of the three groups
will be doing the charcoal

00:43:58.770 --> 00:43:59.887
trainings in the field.

00:43:59.887 --> 00:44:01.470
So just to start out
with, do you guys

00:44:01.470 --> 00:44:02.910
have any questions
about how you'll

00:44:02.910 --> 00:44:03.952
be running that training?

00:44:03.952 --> 00:44:07.050
Have thought about
it at all yet?

00:44:07.050 --> 00:44:09.610
How are you feeling
about doing that?

00:44:09.610 --> 00:44:10.880
Yeah?

00:44:10.880 --> 00:44:14.683
STUDENT: How do we know
what material [INAUDIBLE]??

00:44:14.683 --> 00:44:15.350
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:44:15.350 --> 00:44:19.520
So your mentor, your trip
leader has the contact

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:20.690
for your community partner.

00:44:20.690 --> 00:44:22.190
And so you guys
should be in contact

00:44:22.190 --> 00:44:24.380
with them to find
out what is there

00:44:24.380 --> 00:44:27.650
and what needs to be brought.

00:44:27.650 --> 00:44:30.080
And you should also be reminding
them right now that they

00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:32.960
need raw material, the waste.

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:36.858
They should be gathering
that and drying it right now.

00:44:36.858 --> 00:44:38.150
Any other thoughts or comments?

00:44:42.740 --> 00:44:45.080
So just one thing
to think about is

00:44:45.080 --> 00:44:48.470
what's the balance between
you doing a demonstration

00:44:48.470 --> 00:44:52.278
and you having the people
there actually getting involved

00:44:52.278 --> 00:44:54.320
and getting their hands
dirty and doing the work?

00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:59.840
And how do you balance that both
from a pedagogical standpoint,

00:44:59.840 --> 00:45:03.380
how do you help them learn
this activity the best,

00:45:03.380 --> 00:45:05.930
and then also--

00:45:05.930 --> 00:45:09.350
So making sure you have the dry
waste that's truly dry is huge.

00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:14.690
Loading the drum, not getting
cut, because people often get

00:45:14.690 --> 00:45:16.580
cut in that step.

00:45:16.580 --> 00:45:18.770
Making sure you really
think before you light

00:45:18.770 --> 00:45:23.630
the drum on fire is critical
as well as making sure things

00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:25.400
are kind of calm
during the fire,

00:45:25.400 --> 00:45:27.440
because oftentimes things
get a little hectic.

00:45:27.440 --> 00:45:29.632
It's exciting when
there's fire and things

00:45:29.632 --> 00:45:30.590
can get out of control.

00:45:30.590 --> 00:45:33.410
And we've had issues where
a drum got knocked over

00:45:33.410 --> 00:45:36.440
onto its side when we were
covering it up because people

00:45:36.440 --> 00:45:38.640
were just too crazy.

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:42.930
And so it's not insanely
dangerous, but it is dangerous.

00:45:42.930 --> 00:45:44.600
And so it's worth
just making sure you

00:45:44.600 --> 00:45:46.868
have that under control.

00:45:46.868 --> 00:45:48.410
Crushing is pretty
easy when you have

00:45:48.410 --> 00:45:50.910
a big group because everyone
takes a turn jumping on the bag

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:53.270
and that's really nice.

00:45:53.270 --> 00:45:56.030
Making sure you're going
to heat the water with--

00:45:56.030 --> 00:45:59.750
if you're going to heat the
wider with the charcoal,

00:45:59.750 --> 00:46:04.190
with the burn, make sure
you have that prepared.

00:46:04.190 --> 00:46:05.550
Yeah, those are the big ones.

00:46:05.550 --> 00:46:06.860
And then making sure
everyone gets a chance

00:46:06.860 --> 00:46:08.750
to use the briquette press
because some people get really

00:46:08.750 --> 00:46:10.940
into it and don't want
to give up a turn.

00:46:10.940 --> 00:46:13.470
So making sure you do that.

00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:16.160
All right, so the rest of the
class is just time for you guys

00:46:16.160 --> 00:46:16.820
to work.

00:46:16.820 --> 00:46:20.690
I want to remind you to
hand in these four documents

00:46:20.690 --> 00:46:24.080
so that you can travel.

00:46:24.080 --> 00:46:26.840
And then just don't forget
to do your [INAUDIBLE] cards.

00:46:26.840 --> 00:46:30.970
Are there any questions before
you guys get into groups?

00:46:30.970 --> 00:46:33.000
OK, cool.

